WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everybody,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.020
I am delighted to be here

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.180
and grateful to all of you

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.100
for joining us for this discussion.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.060
In 2015,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.340
I gave a TEDx Talk

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.380
in a little elementary school auditorium,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.660
and much to my surprise,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.100
the talk became one
of the 10 most-viewed talks

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.660
in the history of TED.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.700
And in that talk, I conveyed
one simple scientific finding.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.380
The finding that when we study
hundreds of people

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.740
over their entire adult lives,

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.220
the people who turn out
to be the happiest and the healthiest

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.700
are those who have good,
warm connections to others.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.820
So today, I want to take you
deeper into this whole subject,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.220
by exploring how relationships
matter in our lives,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.100
how they affect our health,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.260
what kinds of relationships
give us this big benefit in happiness,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.700
and which tools you can start using today
to make your relationship stronger.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.620
So I do direct this Harvard Study
of Adult Development.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.060
It's, as far as we know,
the longest study of the same people

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.220
that's ever been done,
following people since 1938.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.540
From adolescence
all the way through old age,

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.140
and now following all of their children,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.940
thousands of lives.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.620
And we began to find,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.620
about 30 years ago,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:57.060
this startling connection
between warm relationships

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.260
and how good our lives feel to us,

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.500
our well-being,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:07.700
and also the fact that warm relationships
seemed to keep people

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.340
both physically stronger
and kept their brains sharper

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.860
as they grew older.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.980
And we didn't believe the data at first.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:19.900
We thought, how could this be

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.540
that relationships actually
get into our bodies

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.100
and shape our health?

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.220
But then other studies
began to find the same thing.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.260
We found that people had less depression,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.700
they were less likely to get
diabetes and heart disease,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.660
that they recovered faster from illness

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.140
when they had better connections
with other people.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.300
So then the question is:
How could this work?

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.140
How do relationships shape our happiness
and our physical health?

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.220
Well, one of the best theories,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.820
for which there's now
some pretty good evidence,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.060
is based on the idea of stress.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.060
That, as we know, stress is an inevitable
part of all of our lives.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.620
Stress happens to us every day.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.660
And what we find
is that good relationships

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.980
turn out to be stress regulators.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.380
So let me give you an example.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.460
Let's say that I have something upsetting
happen to me during the day,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.780
and I find myself, like,
ruminating about it

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.300
and really thinking about it and unhappy.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.380
I can feel my body go into
what we call fight or flight response

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.460
where literally my heart rate goes up

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.180
and I might start sweating a little bit

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.420
and I'm just not feeling as well.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:46.580
Now, what we're meant to do
is to come back to equilibrium

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.860
when a stressor goes away.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.420
That's the way the body
is supposed to work.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:58.380
But what happens if I go home
at the end of my upsetting day

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.220
and I have somebody to talk to?

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.300
Either I can call someone on the phone
or it's somebody I live with.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.380
I can literally feel my body calm down.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.900
I can feel that fight or flight
response subside.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.580
But what if I don't have
anybody to go home to?

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.020
What if there's nobody I can call?

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:25.180
What we find is that people
who are isolated, are lonely,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:30.700
don't have those stress regulators
that we get from good relationships

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.220
and that we stay in chronic
fight or flight mode,

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.740
that our bodies have this chronic stress,

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.060
chronic levels of inflammation

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.140
and circulating stress hormones
that wear away our happiness

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.380
and break down different body systems.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.820
Well, what kinds of relationships
seem essential to well-being?

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.940
And this is interesting.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.660
We asked people

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.140
who were our original
participants in our study.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.340
We asked them,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:12.820
Who could you call in the middle
of the night if you were sick or scared?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.580
And many of our people could list
several other people they could call

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.340
if they were in trouble.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.860
Some people couldn't list anyone.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.580
There wasn't a person on the planet
who they could turn to

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.700
if they were sick or scared.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.100
And what we find is that having
at least one person in your life

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.260
who you feel really has your back,

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.260
who you could go to
if you were in trouble,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:43.300
that's essential for maintaining
our happiness and our health.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.540
When we asked these same people,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.780
when they got to be in their 80s,

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.820
to look back on their lives

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.380
and to tell us what they were proudest of,

00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:01.620
almost everybody said something
about their relationships.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.780
They didn't say, "I made a lot of money"

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.420
or "I won some big awards."

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.340
They said,

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.740
"I was a good mentor,"

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.340
"I was a good friend,"

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.500
"I raised healthy kids,"

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.140
"I was a good partner."

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.340
And so what we find

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.860
is that what seems to mean
the most to people

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.060
when they get to the end of their lives

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:27.220
is the strength and the warmth
of their connections to others.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.500
So then the question comes up, well,

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.860
which types of relationships
support our well-being?

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.660
Some people have asked,

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:41.940
"Do I need to be in an intimate
relationship to get this benefit?"

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.220
Absolutely not.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:47.140
All types of relationships
support our well-being.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.940
So friendships, relatives,

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:53.100
work colleagues, casual contacts.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:56.260
The person who gets you
your coffee every morning

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.740
at Starbucks or Dunkin' Donuts,

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.780
the person who checks you out
in the grocery store,

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.220
who you see maybe every week.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.540
Even talking to strangers
has that benefit.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:11.620
So they did an experiment

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.260
where they assigned some people
who were about to go on the subway

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:18.100
the task of talking to a stranger

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.060
while other people
were assigned the task

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:23.300
of just doing their usual thing
of being on their phones

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.420
or listening to music or reading.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.300
It turned out that the people
who were assigned to talk to strangers

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.620
didn't think they were going to like it,

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.740
but they turned out to be much happier
at the end of the task

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.820
than the people who just rode the subway
keeping to themselves.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.300
So even talking to strangers
gives us that little hit of well-being

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:46.900
from relationships.

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.180
So the question comes up:

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.220
How can we strengthen
our connections with other people?

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.900
And this is where we've come
to think about it

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.380
as a kind of social fitness.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:03.100
If we think about physical fitness,

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.780
you know, we we think,
OK, I'll go to the gym,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.060
I’ll work out, I’ll take a long walk,

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.420
I'll do something to keep
myself strong and fit.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.060
But then we come home
and we don't say, I'm done,

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.060
I don't ever have to do that again.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.540
We have the sense
that physical fitness is a practice

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.700
that we need to maintain over time.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:26.180
It turns out that social
fitness is the same.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.020
That in fact, our friendships,

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.180
our relationships don't just
take care of themselves,

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:35.260
that even good relationships
need tending to,

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.820
they need attention.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.700
They need returning to them
over and over again.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:44.420
So what are some ways that we can
strengthen our relationships?

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.780
Well, one way is to be proactive,
to take the initiative.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:51.580
So to reach out to a friend

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.620
and ask her to take a walk

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.100
instead of spending
two more hours on your laptop

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:00.860
this weekend on Saturday afternoon.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:04.060
Establish some routines with the people

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.620
who are most important to you.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:09.420
A regular phone call
or a coffee every Saturday

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:13.100
with someone you really want
to be sure you see regularly.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.260
Or meeting somebody at the gym.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.220
Or having lunch with a coworker.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:24.220
The other thing we can do is liven up
those long-standing relationships,

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:26.620
particularly like the people we live with.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.620
You know, people we can come
to take for granted,

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.380
by proposing to do something new.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.020
Going out on a date,

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.540
just taking a walk,
if that's not your usual routine.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:43.660
The other thing that we know
works to help people,

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.460
particularly who aren't as connected
to others as they want to be,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.620
is to connect around shared interests.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:53.420
So volunteer in the community

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:56.660
to do something that you care about.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.700
It might be a gardening club,
it might be a bowling league,

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.660
it might be a political cause.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.580
But to do something that you care about

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:07.220
because then you're with other people
who care about the same thing,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.300
and that's a natural place
to start conversations

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.020
that can lead to ongoing relationships.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.300
And finally,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.380
get more comfortable striking up
casual conversations.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.660
That's something we can learn
to be more comfortable with,

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.380
almost like exercising a muscle.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.580
So now,

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:36.620
I would like to ask you
to do one of these things,

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.660
to make one of these choices
that we can make every day.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:43.780
I'd like you to make
that choice right now.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.820
Here’s my challenge to you:

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.940
think about someone you miss,

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.100
that you just haven't seen in a while

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.900
or you haven't had contact with,

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.980
and you'd like to make contact.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.900
You don't even have to have
a particular reason.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:05.860
Now I want you to take out your phone
or whatever device you use, right now,

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.100
and send that person just a little note.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.700
It could say, "I'm just thinking
of you and wanted to connect."

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.620
Or you could say something more personal
to you and to your relationship.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:23.020
And then watch what comes back to you.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:25.860
And while we're here,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.660
we may even have time
to share with each other

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:32.740
some of the responses
that people get when they do this.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.420
So think of someone.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.820
And make contact right now.

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:43.500
The point of this simple exercise

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:47.180
is to remind us that even small actions

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:51.940
can have ripple effects
that build our well-being.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:56.980
And that these are things you can do
every day in the moment.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.380
Thank you.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.580
Whitney Pennington Rodgers:
One way I'd love to kick things off,

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.540
Bob, you mentioned,
at the top of your talk,

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.620
that you run this Harvard Study
of Adult Development

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.820
and gave us sort of a snippet
of what that is.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:14.780
But could you tell us a little bit
more about the study,

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.060
what does it track?

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:18.700
Why did it start,
who’s in the study today?

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:20.860
Robert Waldinger: Absolutely.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.900
The study started in 1938,

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.020
and it started with two groups:

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.180
a group of Harvard College undergrads,

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.580
and it started with a group
of inner city boys

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:36.060
who were in elementary school
or middle school

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.380
from really disadvantaged,
troubled families.

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:41.900
And each study was trying to look

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:45.780
at how people can take
healthy developmental paths.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:49.900
And so the idea was not to study
what goes wrong in our lives

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:51.700
but what goes right in our lives.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.300
And some of the factors that contribute
to helping things go well

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.140
in human development.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:02.300
Needless to say, we started
out with boys, all males,

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.540
but we’ve added women.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.340
And we have more than half women now,

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:08.220
and we've added the second generation.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.460
So we started out with 724 people.

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.940
Now we have over 2,000 people in our study

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.500
and we're still collecting data today.

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:19.140
WPR: Wow.

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:22.260
And I know in the book you talk about ...

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:27.100
how the advice you offer,

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:30.060
the wisdom you offer is not just
drawn from your own study

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.060
because of some of what you've suggested,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:36.340
that you're just now starting to bring
women and different generations.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.940
And I guess could you talk
about some of the other gaps

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.420
and why it's been important
to also think about some

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:44.540
of the other studies out there
on life and happiness?

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:47.060
RW: Yes, that's a really good point,

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:51.260
because, particularly
in this kind of research,

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.940
no single study is proof of anything.

00:13:54.940 --> 00:14:00.020
That what we want is for different
studies of different populations.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.140
So different ethnic groups,
different cultural groups,

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.380
different geographies, right?

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:09.540
We want different studies
to point in the same direction.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:13.460
And that's why I'm here
and I can tell you with confidence,

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:18.380
many studies point to all these same
benefits of relationships.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:24.260
WPR: And I know you touched
on the health element in your talk

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:27.260
and shared that there is data
that reveals that we have --

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.220
that there's a strong connection
between happiness and health.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:34.700
What have you specifically found
related to happiness and health outcomes?

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:40.940
RW: What we find is that happiness

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:45.100
turns out to make us age more slowly

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:48.860
and keep us able longer.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:55.060
So the diseases of aging
that happen to all of us, happen later,

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.180
sometimes they don't happen at all

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:01.740
in people who are happier
have a greater sense of well-being.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:05.780
And it's because of what I spoke about
a few minutes ago,

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:11.780
this kind of decrease in chronic
inflammation and chronic stress.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:15.460
And so what we find is
that -- we can't guarantee

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:18.940
that any one person
is going to stay happier or live longer

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:20.620
if they have better relationships,

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:22.740
but we find that there are
these ingredients,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.900
just like taking care of your health --

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:29.060
you know, not smoking,
not abusing alcohol or drugs,

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:31.060
exercising regularly,

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:32.980
getting regular health care,

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:34.740
having access to health care.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:38.820
All of those things
really matter for our health.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.180
WPR: And you start the book
with a question.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.060
You say, "If you had to make
one life choice right now

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.820
to set yourself on the path
to future health and happiness,

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:49.100
what would it be?"

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:50.740
And from hearing your talk today,

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:52.900
we know that the right answer
to that question

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:54.900
is to build more warm connections.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:58.580
But I want to ask you
about the use of the word "choice" there

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:02.660
and understanding more about whether
or not having warm connections

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.820
is a choice that we make

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:06.100
or, to put it another way,

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:12.140
are there qualities that each of us have
that maybe make us more social people

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:14.940
and that these are just baked
into our personalities?

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.420
Can we decide to be a person
who makes connections?

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:24.140
RW: That's a really important
point because we differ.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:29.100
We are all different in how much
connection we want and benefit from.

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:33.380
So some of us are introverts,
and that’s just fine.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:34.660
That's perfectly normal.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.860
And introverts don't want
a lot of connections.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.940
In fact, being with a lot of people
is exhausting for introverts.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:46.500
And so some people just need one
or two good relationships.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.100
Everybody needs a little
bit of connection.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.420
But some people actually don't need a lot.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:58.060
And so what we want to do
is try to figure out for ourselves

00:16:58.100 --> 00:16:59.980
what's right for me.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:04.420
And that involves, really,
just tuning in and saying,

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:07.740
"OK, I'm energized
by being with a lot of people,

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:09.060
so I'm going to do that,"

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:12.380
or "No, I'm exhausted
by being with a lot of people.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:17.020
For me, you know, a quiet
conversation with one person

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:21.860
is the most energizing thing
I can do in the interpersonal realm."

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:27.420
And so it's really a matter of discerning
what's right for each person.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:32.020
WPR: And what factors
have you found contribute to that?

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:34.300
Maybe things from early
in life or childhood

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:39.820
that might actually shape our ability
to make these connections later in life?

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:45.100
RW: Yes, so, many of our social skills
we learn when we're growing up.

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:47.100
We learn it in our families,

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:50.900
we learn it at school
and on the playground.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:54.860
They can be learned,
and they can be improved

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:56.300
as we get into adulthood.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.900
They’re not set in stone
once we’re done with childhood.

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:05.300
So it's really important to see
the ways that, you know,

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:07.300
just like some of the suggestions I made,

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:09.940
ways that you can practice
getting better at this,

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.820
because it really pays off.

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:15.220
But some people are not so good at this.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.580
And they can have perfectly wonderful,

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.340
happy, healthy lives

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:22.500
without being social
butterflies and extroverts.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:23.900
And I just want to name that

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.940
so that people don't go away thinking,

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.420
"Gosh, if I'm not an extrovert,
I'm out of luck."

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:31.020
Not true at all.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:35.420
Many of our happiest people
had quieter lives.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:42.420
WPR: And you know, I guess to the tips
that you offered in the talk, you know,

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.260
you talked about how we can build
warm connections,

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.140
steps that we can take.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:49.020
And this idea of social fitness.

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:54.140
And so I'm curious how can we
actually assess our social fitness,

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.340
and as you said, to sort of,
understand what is right for us

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.540
in terms of how many friends to have.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:03.300
And is it possible
to sustain warm relationships

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:07.420
if you aren't really intentional
in thinking about your social fitness?

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:14.980
RW: That question of how do we assess
our social fitness,

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:17.340
it's really checking in and saying,

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:20.940
am I as connected as I would like to be

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:22.420
to other people?

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:25.220
And if I'm not, what am I missing?

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:29.380
So not all relationships provide us
with the same stuff.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:33.900
Some relationships are relationships
we have because they're fun,

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.100
you know?

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:37.900
And so they're the people
I like to go out and party with.

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:43.380
Some relationships are the relationships
that help me when I'm scared or hurting

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:44.700
or worried about something.

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:47.260
And I need someone
to talk things over with.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.820
I need a sounding board, a good advisor.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.980
Some relationships
are the people who, you know,

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:56.540
loan me tools when I need to fix something
and I don't have the right tool

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:58.460
or give me a ride to the doctor.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:02.100
So part of it is checking in
about, what am I missing?

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.500
And then seeing what might be possible

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:10.740
in terms of developing
more of the relationships

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:13.300
that give us the things we want more of.

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:19.060
WPR: And along those same lines,
TED Member Tiana wants to know:

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:22.420
How do we define if a relationship
is actually meaningful or not?

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:25.620
I mean, it sounds like understanding
what these voices are saying,

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:29.420
but are there other things we should pay
attention to, to know if it's valuable,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:31.020
if this is a warm connection?

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.700
RW: One way to think about it

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.740
is not whether the relationship
is smooth all the time,

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:40.940
but whether we feel
that we can be authentic,

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.500
we can be ourselves in a relationship.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:47.500
And obviously, we won't be the same selves

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:52.860
in a work relationship that we might be
with our, you know, beloved sister,

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:54.460
you know.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:59.900
But still, can I be myself
in the most essential ways?

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.460
Can I express myself?

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:04.420
And can the other person
express themselves?

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:06.580
Because I think that's where --

00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:09.260
what we really want is to feel known

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:11.300
and to feel seen

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:17.780
and not to feel like we are having
to maintain a façade

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:19.820
that hides who we are.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:24.620
And so the best relationships are those
where we feel we can be ourselves.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:30.860
WPR: And you talked about how, really,
it's romantic relationships, friendships,

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:32.060
acquaintances,

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:34.060
I mean, all of these relationships matter.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:38.380
But do you find that one of these is maybe
more significant than the other,

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.140
or how do you look at them?

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:46.500
RW: I look at it in terms
of the secure attachment we think about.

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:52.220
Attachment is a word for being warmly,
securely connected.

00:21:52.220 --> 00:21:56.020
It’s the “Who can you call in the middle
of the night if you’re sick or scared?”

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:57.780
So I think of it in those terms,

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:03.140
not necessarily the person's role,
defined role in our life,

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:07.780
like romantic partner or boss.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:11.900
But it’s the “Does this person
have my back?”

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:16.220
And so that's, I think,
the defining element

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:19.500
that we want to find
somewhere in our world.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:25.180
WPR: So we have so many
member questions coming in, Bob,

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:28.580
and I'm going to kind of go through
a few more of them.

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:32.500
So TED Member Nancy wants to know
if you think it’s possible

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:37.820
for an endemically unhappy person
at some point in life to obtain happiness.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:40.580
RW: Yes. And thank you
for asking that question.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.020
So we have a couple
of life stories in our book,

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:47.500
the book contains real stories
of real people.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:51.180
The names are disguised
to protect confidentiality.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:58.180
But some of those stories are of people
who have big turnarounds late in life,

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:01.300
people who were isolated and less happy

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:04.220
and in their 60s found a whole community.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:06.420
One man found a community at a gym

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:08.420
that he never dreamed he would find.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:14.300
And so the message that we get
from studying these thousands of lives is:

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.420
it is never too late.

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:19.500
And so don't assume
that it's too late for you,

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:24.020
even if you feel like you've not had
good luck with this in the past.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.780
WPR: Well, Bob, we're getting
some questions about you.

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:29.220
And I want to turn to you for a second.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:35.060
And I'm curious how running this study
has changed the way you approach life

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.060
and your own pursuit of happiness.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.460
RW: Oh, gosh.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:44.140
Well, one of the things
it's helped me with is the idea

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:48.100
that everybody
has struggles in their life.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.020
That has been so meaningful to me

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:53.140
because I can also look
at the world and say,

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:55.860
"Gee, other people seem to have lives

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:58.140
that have it all figured out
and perfect lives."

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:00.740
It is so helpful to know

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:02.820
that there isn't a person on the planet

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:04.340
who doesn't have struggles.

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:08.860
And it makes me feel less alone
when I can recognize that.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:12.500
And that's one of the reasons why I want
to keep getting these messages out,

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.740
after having studied thousands of lives.

00:24:15.780 --> 00:24:20.900
And I think the other thing
that it's changed is my own priorities.

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.980
So I realize,

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:28.220
OK, I can sit here this afternoon
and edit another paper

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.780
or do more work,

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:33.300
or I can see my friend
who I haven't seen in a month.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:38.260
And so I’ve become more active
in taking care of my relationships,

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:40.380
and it makes a big difference.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.620
So I would say that those
are the two big things for me

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:47.540
that directing this study
has changed about my life.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.060
WPR: We have a question
from TED Member Nesa.

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:52.740
They ask: “As a mother
of two very young children,

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:55.220
what advice do you have
for parents to build strong,

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:56.460
healthy relationships,

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.820
now through adulthood?"

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:03.620
RW: For parents,

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:10.060
it’s really helping your children
to pay attention

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:12.740
to how they feel

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:18.100
and to learn to use their feelings
with more choice.

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:22.780
So learning to love what they love
and hang on to what they love,

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:24.740
even if it's not so popular.

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:28.100
You know, I had a mother talk to me

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:32.540
about how her child really loved
doing improv comedy.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:34.100
And he’s only 12,

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:35.860
and his friends don't like that.

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:39.180
And so we talked about how do you help
your child feel supported

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:42.860
in loving what he loves
and taking an improv class, right?

00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:46.660
So helping your kids learn
that it's OK to love something

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:50.380
that's not the same
as all the other kids in their group

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:52.100
and keep loving what they love.

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:56.180
And helping kids learn
that it’s OK to be unhappy

00:25:56.180 --> 00:25:57.940
and that that will pass,

00:25:57.980 --> 00:25:59.900
that feelings come and go.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.500
That it’s OK to disagree.

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:04.620
Families can model,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.620
parents can model for kids.

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:08.380
We can have disagreements.

00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:13.740
We can work it out and and hold on
to these good relationships

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:15.420
even through disagreements.

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:19.620
Those are some of the biggest lessons
we can help our children with,

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:21.540
about how to use their feelings

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:24.420
rather than kind of,
be buffeted by their feelings

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.420
and ruled by their feelings.

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:30.180
WPR: I'm curious just where you see
the study going from here.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:34.460
RW: We are collecting more data,
even as we speak.

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:38.540
We are collecting more information
from the children.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:41.420
Almost all the original
participants have passed away,

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:44.420
but their children are all
Baby Boomers, on average.

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:46.940
And so we're collecting information,

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:50.860
including about what life was like
during the pandemic.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:55.380
Also collecting information
about how they use social media,

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:58.380
which is something we've all been
talking about a bit here.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:02.380
And I think in terms of the future,

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:06.900
we see ourselves as wanting
to make our data available

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:08.820
to more and more researchers.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.180
So we want to collaborate,
and we do collaborate,

00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:14.020
with other research groups.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:16.620
We say, come in and use our data

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:20.780
and ask new questions
that we don’t even think to ask.

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:25.100
Because we have this treasure trove
of information about thousands of lives.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:31.260
And then we're going to make it
publicly available on public websites,

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:33.180
because, you know, much of our work

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:35.660
has been funded by the federal government,

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:38.340
by NIH, with taxpayer money.

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:43.100
And so we feel a responsibility
to make this information available

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:45.460
to other researchers

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:49.140
who want to ask their own
questions about our data.

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:54.620
WPR: Well, Bob, you’ve shared
so many great things with us.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.820
You know, I feel like
if people were listening

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:00.700
and trying to absorb all of it,

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:02.660
and at the very end of this conversation

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:06.660
you want just one nugget of information,
that if you missed everything,

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:10.420
what is the one thing that you want
everyone to walk away from,

00:28:10.460 --> 00:28:12.100
at the end of this conversation?

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:18.340
RW: That if you want to make
one choice today

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:21.660
that will make you healthier and happier,

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:26.540
it is to pay attention to improving
your connections with other people.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:31.980
That that is such a good investment,
and it will pay off for years to come.

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:34.980
